\ul{
\li{Defined the lattice and #{\II}-algebra structures on #{n}-simplices and simplified
the lattice structure on the [polynomial lattice](stt-009W).}
\li{Constructed [slice algebras](stt-00UQ) and [opposite algebras](stt-00TG).}
\li{Defined [conjunctive types](stt-00TW), the dual to [disjunctive types](stt-00DY).}
\li{Showed that if [finite-dimensional cubes separate](stt-00J7),
the global points of #{\Delta^1} are the booleans, and the interval is
[directed](stt-00VP), then the interval has the
[disjunctive property](Synthetic.DisjunctiveProperty).}
}

\p{One nice observation made during the workshop was that we can take advantage
of the module system and the fact that our interval is a 'regular' type (not built-in)
to formalise dual concepts by instantiating modules with the opposite lattice. This
is particularly useful because we don't necessarily assume that we have opposite
categories. This can be seen in the module \ref{Synthetic.Conjunctive} which simply
reexports \ref{Synthetic.Disjunctive}. Currently, whilst our definition
of [lattice](stt-009T) is judgmentally involutive, it fails to satisfy the law
#{(i \le_{A} j) = (j \le_{A^{\op}} i)} \em{syntactically}.
This is because #{\le} is defined in terms of #{\land} as opposed to being a part
of the data. This lack of judgmental equality may make modules instantiated with the
opposite lattice awkward to use. This can be fixed by adding a dedicated field for
#{\le}, satisfying the conditions
#{(i \le j) \iff (i \land j = i)} and #{(i \le j) \iff (i \lor j = j)}.}


\p{Since the workshop [[ecavallo]] has continued developing lattice algebra, working
towards a proof that simplices are affine, meaning they arise as the spectrum of an
#{\II}-algebra. This can be used in combination with the quasicoherence principle to
show that every #{\Delta^n} is a category.}

